re. * The "poison" (the antibodies) was removed, but the "poison factory" (the immune system cells in the lymph nodes and bone marrow) is still there. * To keep the patient from getting sick again, doctors usually have to give them medications (immunosuppressants) to tell the immune system to stop making those bad antibodies. * *(Note: Some acute autoimmune conditions, like Guillain-Barré, do go away and don't come back after treatment, but chronic ones require ongoing management).* ### 4. Why This Still Doesn't Work for HIV or Cancer This brings us back to the mechanics of HIV and cancer. Plasma exchange works for autoimmune diseases because the immediate danger (the antibodies) is floating freely in the liquid of the blood. * **If you try this on HIV:** The HIV virus isn't just floating in the plasma. It is permanently fused into the DNA of the patient's **white blood cells**. Because a plasma exchange returns the patient's white blood cells back to their body, the HIV goes right back in with them. * **If you try this on Cancer:** Cancer is made of mutated cells forming tumors in the organs or bone marrow. Filtering the liquid plasma does absolutely nothing to a tumor growing in a lung, or to the bone marrow pumping out leukemia cells. **The Summary:** You are absolutely right that we can "wash" or swap parts of the blood to treat specific illnesses! Plasmapheresis is an amazing medical marvel. But it acts like a high-tech water filter. A water filter is great if you need to remove dirt floating in the water (autoimmune antibodies). But it won't help if the pipes themselves are rusted (cancer), or if the water source has been fundamentally altered at the molecular level (HIV).
